### Before: Theo's personal token, as the earlier case captured it, reads his job — so the 401 below is the removal,
### not a token that never worked
# @name TheosTokenBefore
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{TheoToken}}

### Dana removes Theo from Acme
# @name DanaRemovesTheo
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/auth/v1/tenants/{{AcmeId}}/members/{{TheoId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{DanaAccess}}

### Theo's very next request in Acme, with an access token minted while he was a member. Naming a tenant he no longer
### belongs to refuses the whole principal — never a quieter, tenantless one.
# @name TheoInAcmeAfter
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [401]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{AcmeId}}

### His personal token acts in Acme as him; he is no longer there, so it is no longer anything
# @name TheosTokenAfter
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [401]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{TheoToken}}

### But he is still signed in: only the membership ended
# @name TheoUserAfter
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/auth/v1/user
Authorization: Bearer {{TheoAccess}}

### Billing still reads: a service token stands on its minter's power, and Dana is still Acme's owner
# @name BillingStillReads
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?status=eq.completed&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{BillingToken}}
